 "title": "Number of Connected Components In An Undirected Graph",
 "slug": "number-of-connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph",
 "category": "graphs",
 "difficulty": "Medium",
 "url": "https://leetcode.com/problems/number-of-connected-components-in-an-undirected-graph/",
 "pattern": "DFS from every unvisited node",
 "statement": "There are `n` nodes labelled `0 … n − 1` and a list of undirected `edges`. Return the number of **connected components** (groups of nodes that are linked to each other, directly or through other nodes).",
 "example": {
  "input": "n = 5, edges = [[0,1],[1,2],[3,4]]",
  "output": "2",
  "why": "Nodes 0, 1, 2 form one group and nodes 3, 4 another."
 },
 "more": [
  {
   "input": "n = 5, edges = [[0,1],[1,2],[2,3],[3,4]]",
   "output": "1"
  },
  {
   "input": "n = 4, edges = []",
   "output": "4"
  },
  {
   "input": "n = 1, edges = []",
   "output": "1"
  },
  {
   "input": "n = 6, edges = [[0,1],[2,3],[4,5],[1,2]]",
   "output": "2"
  }
 ],
 "idea": [
  "Build an adjacency list so that every node knows its neighbours.",
  "Go through the nodes in order. A node that has **not been visited** yet belongs to a component nobody has seen — count one more.",
  "Then run a DFS from it to mark its **whole** component as visited, so none of its nodes is counted again."
 ],
 "code": "from collections import defaultdict, deque\n\nclass Solution:\n    def countComponents(self, n: int, edges: List[List[int]]) -> int:\n        graph = defaultdict(list)\n        for u, v in edges:\n            graph[u].append(v)\n            graph[v].append(u)\n\n        def dfs(node):\n            visited.add(node)\n            for neighbor in graph[node]:\n                if neighbor not in visited:\n                    dfs(neighbor)\n\n        visited = set()\n        components = 0\n\n        for node in range(n):\n            if node not in visited:\n                components += 1\n                dfs(node)\n\n        return components",
 "complexity": {
  "time": "O(V + E)",
  "timeWhy": "Every node is visited once and every edge is looked at from both ends.",
  "space": "O(V + E)",
  "spaceWhy": "The adjacency list, the visited set and the recursion stack."
 },
